# Kotlin Gray-Part — User-Agent

## 1. One builder, one string

`core/UserAgent.value` is the only place a User-Agent is constructed.
Everything that talks to a server reads from it:

- the config endpoint POST (`ReachDispatch`)
- the AppsFlyer GCD fallback GET (`TrackingDispatch`)
- the WebView (`StreamPortal`, via `settings.userAgentString`)

**Why this matters and is not cosmetic.** The previous version of this
template had two independent builders — the router's said
`Chrome/131.0.0.0`, the WebView's said `Chrome/131.0.6778.135`. The backend
made its gray/white decision against one UA and the partner site then saw a
different one. Some partner cashiers key session validity on the UA and were
dropping the session on the first navigation. If you add a third caller, read
from `UserAgent.value`; never assemble a string locally.

## 2. What the string looks like

```
Mozilla/5.0 (Linux; Android <release>; <BRAND> <MODEL> Build/<ID>)
AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/<major>.0.<build>.<patch>
Mobile Safari/537.36
```

- `<release>`, `<BRAND>`, `<MODEL>`, `<ID>` come from `android.os.Build` at
  runtime — real device values, filtered to printable ASCII and with spaces in
  the model replaced by `_`, exactly as Chrome does it.
- `<major>.0.<build>.<patch>` is **per project**, derived from `gray.seed` and
  delivered through `BuildConfig.UA_CHROME_MAJOR/BUILD/PATCH`. Ranges:
  major 146–150, build 6900–7900, patch 40–250.

### On the scaffolding literals

`Mozilla/5.0 (Linux; Android `, `AppleWebKit/537.36`, `Mobile Safari/537.36`
appear as plain literals in `core/UserAgent.kt`, and that is correct. These
are what every real Chrome on Android sends; they are universal, not a
portfolio marker. What clusters a portfolio is the **version tuple** — which
is why that part is seeded and the scaffolding is not.

This is a deliberate divergence from the iOS rule
(`apple_moderation_hardening.mdc` §4), which requires encoding the scaffolding
too. Apple's scanner indexes the scaffolding substring in Mach-O; Google's
does not, and encoding a string that a billion honest apps also contain buys
nothing while adding decoder infrastructure to the binary.

## 3. Never write these

- **No `Dart`, `Flutter`, `okhttp`, `WebView`, or the library default.** The
  OkHttp default (`okhttp/4.12.0`) on the config POST is an immediate tell
  that the request is not from a browser. `ReachDispatch` sets the header
  explicitly for exactly this reason — do not remove it.
- **No `; wv)` token.** Android's stock WebView UA includes `; wv` inside the
  platform parentheses. Chrome proper does not. We present as Chrome, so the
  token is absent — `settings.userAgentString` is overwritten before the first
  load.
- **No hardcoded device model.** A fixed `SM-G991B` across a portfolio is a
  perfect join.

## 4. The `appid/appname` suffix — decision matrix

Some partner backends route the cashier on a suffix appended to the UA:

```
… Mobile Safari/537.36 appid/<applicationId> appname/<Token>
```

**No honest browser writes this.** It is the single most distinctive string
the app can send, and the exact pattern appears on affiliate templates. It is
off by default (`gray.uaAppSuffix = false`).

| Situation | What to do |
|---|---|
| Partner does not need it | Leave it off. This is the default and the right answer |
| Partner accepts a custom **request header** instead | Send `X-App-Id` on the config POST; leave the UA clean |
| Partner accepts the identity **out of band** (per-app endpoint, query param) | Use that; leave the UA clean |
| Partner genuinely will not budge | Set `gray.uaAppSuffix = true` and `gray.uaAppToken = <Token>`, and note it in the sign-off table |

When it is on, both tokens vary per project anyway — `applicationId` is
project-specific and `gray.uaAppToken` is a per-project string — so two apps
never send the same suffix. That mitigates the join but not the "no browser
writes this" tell. Prefer a header.

## 5. Changing the UA per project

Nothing to do by hand. Rotate `gray.seed`; the version tuple moves with it.

```powershell
gradlew grayReport      # prints "chrome UA = 148.0.7203.171"
```

Diff that line against every sibling project. If it matches, the seed was
reused and the whole fingerprint is shared, not just the UA.

## 6. Verification

```powershell
# No second UA builder anywhere.
rg -n 'Mozilla/5\.0' app/src/main/java        # exactly one hit: core/UserAgent.kt

# No library default leaking onto the config POST.
rg -n 'User-Agent' app/src/main/java          # ReachDispatch + TrackingDispatch set it

# Suffix state matches the partner agreement.
rg -n '^gray\.uaAppSuffix' gray.properties
```

On device, confirm what the partner actually receives — the site's own
`navigator.userAgent` in a debug WebView console, or the backend's access log.
An assumption here has cost a full test cycle before.
